+# [MP8] ================ Manufacture Gun (ManufactureGun, mp8game=3) 8-player adaptation ================
+#
+# Scene facts (measured read-only against the stock PCK with tools\probe\probe32/33/34/35, not guessed)
+#
+# 1. **There are three spawn containers, all grabbed with a hard-coded $ in the script, not @export**
+#      $SpawnPositions          player spawn points   4
+#      $WorkstationSpawns       workstation slots     4
+#      $ItemSpawner/ItemSpawns  part spawn points     26
+#
+# 2. **At runtime `empty_desk_array` really has 4 entries** (the scene does override it, pointing at
+#    `empty_desks/desk0..3_empty`, and the four empty desks line up exactly with the four station positions).
+#    `remove_empty_workstation_rpc(counter)` indexes by counter → reads out of bounds for #5~8.
+#    ⚠️ `recipe_indicator_ligths` has 5 entries, but it is indexed by **recipe step** (0~4), not player count, so leave it alone.
+#    `empty_desk_array[...]` is the only index access in the whole file (fully grepped, pitfall #13).
+#
+# 3. ⚠️ **3 of the stations computed by the generic top-up (network_manager's node name allowlist) land inside solid geometry**.
+#    `"workstationspawns"` was originally on the allowlist, and taking the midpoint of adjacent points gives the midpoints of the four edges.
+#      mid(0-1) @ (-7.29, 0.00) → the recipe indicator wall's collision box Box 4×4.5×9, distance 0.00
+#      mid(2-3) @ (10.75, 0.10) → pallet 0.10 + wall collision box 0.34
+#      mid(3-0) @ ( 1.68,-7.88) → center parts table desk left 1.62 (workstation half-width 1.18, so it grazes it)
+#      mid(1-2) @ ( 1.78, 7.95) → desk trolley 1.10 (half depth 1.855 → overlaps by 0.75)
+#    So `"workstationspawns"` has been **removed from the allowlist** (see the comment in network_manager.gd),
+#    and this file's `_mp8_expand_workstations()` places them from the MP8_LAYOUT_RING coordinate table instead.
+#    Player spawn points `"spawnpositions"` still use the generic top-up — its 4 midpoints were all measured to be on open floor.
+#
+# 4. **Too few parts will soft-lock the match** (the most critical item in the 8-player adaptation)
+#    Parts on the field = the 26 ItemSpawns points, one per point, **all consumed once and never respawned**
+#    (`ManufactureGunItem.NonConsumableItems` is an empty array). Every player's recipe is always
+#    one each of 5 variants → with 26 handed out each variant only has 5~6, but 8 players need 8
+#    → **only 5~6 players can build a gun**. And this minigame **has no timeout at all** (play_state is empty,
+#    the Minigame base class has no timer, and game.gd has no fallback either). The only end condition is "living players ≤ 1"
+#    → if the last two living players both lack a gun the match **never ends**. So parts must scale with player count.
+#
+# 5. The camera needs no changes - all 8 stations were measured inside the view frustum (horizontal/vertical share ≤ 0.75).
+
+const MP8_MAX_PLAYERS: int = 8
+
+# ❌ **Abandoned approach - corner pairs (shifted 3.2 m along x) — rejected by the user after testing, don't go back to it.**
+#
+# Symptom - "one table outside, one table inside, and the player at the inside table can't get in at all".
+# Root cause (pinned down with numbers by probe37) - **the camera looks along +X** (camera at (-17.89, 11.95, 0),
+# yaw -90°), so **horizontal screen position ≈ z ÷ depth**. Shifting along x = lining tables up front to back along the line of sight.
+# The two tables share the same z, so horizontally on screen they differ only by a depth ratio.
+#
+#   | | horizontal screen position | gap to neighbour |
+#   | vanilla 4 tables | -0.727 / -0.393 / +0.395 / +0.743 | 0.334 / 0.788 / 0.348 |
+#   | pairs at 3.2 m | each pair differs by only **0.039 ~ 0.117** | — |
+#
+# And one table alone is ±0.18 wide on screen, so the two **overlap by 0.22**, which amounts to one covering the other.
+# Vanilla only reads clearly because each wall holds just 2 tables, placed at the two extremes (depth 14.9 vs 29.7, exactly double).
+#
+# **Lesson (same pattern as pitfall #21)** - in vanilla, "only 2 tables per wall" hid the
+# "screen horizontal = z/depth" constraint. As soon as a 3rd table goes on the same wall, the constraint shows itself.
+# Criterion - **verify placement changes against the screen projection, not just "does it fit on the floor"**. The probe35 round
+# only checked footprint clearance, came back all green, and then in the real game everything was stacked together.
+
+# ---- Current approach - ring — "a ring around the edge, middle all empty", set from the user's hand-drawn sketch on 2026-08-16 ----
+#
+# The user drew it from the **screen view** (recipe display board at the bottom center of the sketch). The direction conversion is calibrated.
+#   Sketch left→right = world z from south to north     sketch top→bottom = world x from far to near (camera at the bottom, looking along +X)
+# Calibration basis - the recipe board's real position is world x≈-8.2, z∈[-4.5,+4.5], and it is drawn at the bottom center of the sketch, so it matches.
+#
+# Layout (checked cell by cell against probe37's live field map)
+#   Top row of 4 - vanilla3(z=-8.2) / B(8,-3) / C(8,+3) / vanilla2(z=+8.4)
+#   Left middle, 1 table A (middle of south wall)   right middle, 1 table D (middle of north wall)   bottom two corners = vanilla0 / vanilla1
+#
+# ⚠️ **B/C sit in the x=+8.0 band, not against the east wall** — in the real game the user spotted
+# "a part respawn point by the wall in the top-right corner", and measuring showed the whole east wall strip x≥10.5 is part points (part_011/012/016/025)
+# plus the pallet pallet_001 and the wall collision box CollisionShape3D2, so a table doesn't fit there and shouldn't take that space.
+# Meanwhile x∈[+6.0,+9.5] is one open stretch, clear all the way from z=-9.5 to +9.5, just right for two tables.
+#
+# Angle 90 = what the user called "rotate 90 degrees". The footprint goes from 2.40(x)×3.80(z) to 3.80×2.40,
+# which is 37% narrower on screen and fits narrow spots that didn't fit before — on the south wall the x∈[-2,+2] stretch at z≤-9.5
+# has wall jutting out, so a table 3.80 deep hits it and only gets through when turned to 2.40. **It isn't just for looks.**
+#
+# Acceptance numbers (probe37) - all 8 positions "fit + are reachable".
+# Minimum 2D screen gap **0.251** (vanilla 4 tables is 0.334, the previous corner-pair version only 0.112).
+# The tightest pair (vanilla3 ↔ B) has combined screen half-widths of 0.153 < 0.251, so they **won't overlap**.
+#
+# Alternative solver - probe37 greedily picked from 978 valid cells by "farthest on screen from the tables already chosen",
+# which gives better spacing (0.310) but is asymmetric and puts one table in the middle of the field. Switch with the command line `mp8gunlayout=solver`.
+#
+# ⚠️ The validity check is calibrated so that **all 4 vanilla positions pass** (took three rounds to get right).
+#
+# ⚠️ On 2026-08-19 the **user's hand-placed final layout from 2026-08-16 22:24** was baked into this table
+# (it used to exist only in `user://mp8_gun_layout.txt` on the host machine, and was the direct cause of this version's 6-player bug
+#  "clients have a table blocking the middle of the path" — see `_mp8_broadcast_layout()` in this file).
+# The old ring from before baking (B/C in the middle of the open area at x=+8.0, the two that blocked the path) is kept for the record.
+#     [Vector3(-1.50, 0.0, -7.00),  90.0]   # A middle of south wall
+#     [Vector3(-3.00, 0.0,  7.60),   0.0]   # D middle of north wall
+#     [Vector3( 8.00, 0.0, -3.00),  90.0]   # B far band · south  ← blocks the path
+#     [Vector3( 8.00, 0.0,  3.00),  90.0]   # C far band · north  ← blocks the path
+# The final layout pushed all four against the walls, leaving the whole walkway in the middle clear.
+const MP8_LAYOUT_RING: Array = [
+	[Vector3(-3.50, 0.0, -10.50), 90.0],   # new1 by the south wall
+	[Vector3(-4.00, 0.0, 10.10), 270.0],   # new2 by the north wall
+	[Vector3(7.03, 0.0, -11.00), 90.0],    # new3 by the south wall · far end
+	[Vector3(11.50, 0.0, 2.50), 0.0],      # new4 by the east wall
+]
+const MP8_LAYOUT_SOLVER: Array = [
+	[Vector3(-1.50, 0.0, -7.00), 90.0],
+	[Vector3(-1.50, 0.0, 8.00), 0.0],
+	[Vector3(3.00, 0.0, 7.00), 90.0],
+	[Vector3(5.50, 0.0, 0.00), 0.0],
+]
